#: ../../source/resolve-url_readme.md:45
msgid "Usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/resolve-url_readme.md:48
msgid "`resolveUrl(...urls)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/resolve-url_readme.md:50
msgid ""
"Pass one or more urls. Resolves the last one to an absolute url, using "
"the previous ones and `window.location`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/resolve-url_readme.md:53
msgid ""
"It’s like starting out on `window.location`, and then clicking links with"
" the urls as `href` attributes in order, from left to right."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/resolve-url_readme.md:56
msgid ""
"Unlike Node.js’ [`path.resolve`], this function always goes through all "
"of the arguments, from left to right. `path.resolve` goes from right to "
"left and only in the worst case goes through them all. Should that "
"matter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/resolve-url_readme.md:60
msgid ""
"Actually, the function is _really_ like clicking a lot of links in "
"series: An actual `<a>` gets its `href` attribute set for each url! This "
"means that the url resolution of the browser is used, which makes this "
"module really light-weight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/resolve-url_readme.md:65
msgid ""
"Also note that this functions deals with urls, not paths, so in that "
"respect it has more in common with Node.js’ [`url.resolve`]. But the "
"arguments are more like [`path.resolve`]."
msgstr ""

